== 9. Secret Duties Are the Most Soul-Enriching

Ninthly, consider that secret duties are the most soul-enriching duties. Just as secret meals make fat bodies, so secret duties make fat souls; and as secret trade brings in great earthly riches, so secret prayers make many rich in spiritual blessings and heavenly riches. Private prayer is that secret key of heaven that unlocks all the treasures of glory to the soul. God usually gives the best riches and the sweetest mercies to His people when they are in their closets on their knees. Just as the warmth the chicks find by sitting close under the hen’s wings cherishes them, so the graces of the saints are enlivened and cherished and strengthened by the sweet secret influences their souls come under when they are in their closet communion with God. Private prayer conscientiously performed is the secret key of heaven that has unlocked such treasures and such secrets as have passed the skill of the craftiest devil to find out. Private prayer is the midwife that brings the choicest mercies and the chiefest riches in to us.

Certainly, no one is so rich in gracious experiences as those who are most exercised in closet duties. “This poor man cried,” says David, “and the #smallcaps[Lord] … saved him from all his troubles” (Ps 34:6). David, pointing to himself, tells us that he “cried,” that is, silently and secretly, as Moses did at the Red Sea, and as Nehemiah did in the presence of the king of Persia. “And the #smallcaps[Lord] … saved him from all his troubles” (v. 6; Exod 14:15; Neh 1:11; 2:4). And oh, what additions these deliverances were to his experiences! O my friends, just as the tender dew that falls in the silent night makes the grass and herbs and flowers flourish and grow more abundantly than great showers of rain that fall in the day, so secret prayer will make the sweet herbs of grace and holiness grow and flourish in the soul more abundantly than all the more open, public, and visible duties of religion, which too, too often are mingled and mixed with the sun and wind of pride and hypocrisy.

Beloved, you#super[+] know that many times a favorite at court gets more by one secret petition, by one private request to his prince, than a tradesman or a merchant gets by twenty years’ labor and pains. So a Christian many times gets more by one secret petition, by one private request to the King of kings, than many others do by trading long in the more public duties of religion. O sirs, remember that in private prayer we have a far greater advantage for the exercise of our own gifts and graces and talents than we have in public; for in public we only hear others exercise their talents and gifts. In public duties we are more passive, but in private duties we are more active. Now, the more our gifts and talents and graces are exercised, the more they are strengthened and increased. All acts strengthen habits. The more sin is acted out, the more it is strengthened. And so it is with our gifts and graces: the more they are exercised, the more they are strengthened.
